    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span class="text-gray-800 font-medium">Oil vs Water-Based Stain Guide</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>🌲 Wood Polymer Chemistry &amp; Coatings Science</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>USDA Forest Products Laboratory Guidelines</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Oil-Based vs. Water-Based Exterior Fence Stain: Penetration, Peeling &amp; Recoat Rules
    </h1>

    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Choosing between an <strong>oil-based penetrating stain</strong> and a <strong>water-based acrylic stain</strong> is the single most critical decision for your exterior wood fence. While water-based stains boast low VOCs and soap-and-water cleanup, oil-based formulas behave fundamentally differently inside wood cell structures—eliminating the risk of future peeling.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Job-Site Photography -->
    <div class="mb-6">
      <img
        src="/images/guides/oil-vs-water-based-exterior-fence-stain.jpg"
        alt="Comparison of oil based penetrating stain on cedar fence vs water based acrylic coating"
        class="w-full h-56 sm:h-72 md:h-84 lg:h-96 object-cover rounded-xl shadow-md border border-gray-200"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- Key Metrics Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 mb-6">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Oil Penetration Depth</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-600">1/16" – 1/8" Deep</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Soaks Inside Wood Pores</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Waterborne Film Mechanism</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-600">Surface Film Barrier</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Sits On Top of Grain</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Long-Term Failure Mode</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">Gradual Fading vs Peeling</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-blue-600">Oil Never Peels or Flakes</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Maintenance Recoat Effort</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">Wash &amp; Recoat</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-purple-600">Zero Sanding Required for Oil</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Deep Dive Content -->
  <div class="prose max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Cellular Absorption vs Surface Film Formation</h2>
    <p>
      The primary difference lies in resin chemistry. Penetrating oil stains use small-molecule drying oils (such as linseed, tung, or synthetic paraffinic oils) dissolved in mineral spirits. These small molecules actively migrate into the tubular wood tracheid cells, displacing trapped air and curing inside the timber. Because there is no plastic film on the surface, moisture vapor can escape naturally as the seasons change.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, waterborne stains utilize large acrylic polymer chains emulsified in water. When the water evaporates, the acrylic resins coalesce into a continuous flexible sheet over the surface. While this provides bright, vivid initial color, outdoor wood continually shrinks and swells with relative humidity. Over 2 to 3 heating seasons, the wood under the acrylic moves more than the surface film can stretch, causing the acrylic to blister, lift, and peel.
    </p>

    <!-- Side-by-Side Comparison Table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-gray-100 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-800 font-semibold">
            <th class="p-3">Performance Characteristic</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-emerald-800">Penetrating Oil-Based Stain</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-blue-800">Water-Based Acrylic Stain</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">Peeling &amp; Flaking Risk</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-emerald-700">0% Risk (Fades Gracefully)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-rose-700">High Risk After 2–3 Years</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">Moisture Tolerance at Application</td>
            <td class="p-3">Requires Dry Wood (&lt;15% MC)</td>
            <td class="p-3">Can Tolerate Damp Wood (&le;22% MC)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">UV Protection Longevity</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-mono">3 – 5 Years (Trans-Oxide Pigments)</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-mono">2 – 3 Years Before Film Cracking</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">Preparation for Recoating</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-emerald-700">Simple Pressure Wash &amp; Re-apply</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-amber-700">Chemical Stripping or Heavy Sanding</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium">Cleanup Solvent</td>
            <td class="p-3">Mineral Spirits / Paint Thinner</td>
            <td class="p-3">Warm Water &amp; Dish Soap</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why Trans-Oxide Pigments Matter in Semi-Transparent Stains</h2>
    <p>
      Clear wood sealers lack UV resistance; sunlight degrades lignin molecules within 6 months, turning the wood gray regardless of oil content. To maintain the golden, amber, or redwood tones of cedar, choose semi-transparent formulations loaded with <strong>trans-oxide iron pigments</strong>. These micro-ground mineral pigments act as microscopic mirrors that reflect ultraviolet light while allowing the natural wood grain and knots to shine through.
    </p>
  </div>

